Here's a simple C++ function that converts a basic **Markdown-formatted string** into **HTML-formatted text**. This implementation supports:

- **Bold (`**text**` → `<strong>text</strong>`**
- **Italic (`*text*` → `<em>text</em>`**
- **Headers (`# Header` → `<h1>Header</h1>`**
- **Unordered lists (`- item` → `<ul><li>item</li></ul>`**

This is a **basic** Markdown parser and doesn't handle all Markdown syntax. For full Markdown support, you'd need a library like **markdown.cpp** or **libmarkdown**.

---

### ✅ C++ Function: Markdown to HTML

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::istringstream iss(markdown);
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(iss, line)) {
        std::string htmlLine;

        // Process headers
        if (line.find('#') == 0) {
            size_t numHashes = 0;
            while (line[numHashes] == '#') ++numHashes;
            if (numHashes <= 6) {
                htmlLine = "<h" + std::to_string(numHashes) + ">" + line.substr(numHashes) + "</h" + std::to_string(numHashes) + ">";
            }
            continue;
        }

        // Process bold and italic
        size_t pos = 0;
        while (pos < line.length()) {
            if (line.substr(pos, 2) == "**") {
                pos += 2;
                size_t end = line.find("**", pos);
                if (end != std::string::npos) {
                    htmlLine += "<strong>" + line.substr(pos, end - pos) + "</strong>";
                    pos = end + 2;
                } else {
                    htmlLine += "<strong>" + line.substr(pos);
                    break;
                }
            } else if (line.substr(pos, 1) == "*") {
                pos += 1;
                size_t end = line.find("*", pos);
                if (end != std::string::npos) {
                    htmlLine += "<em>" + line.substr(pos, end - pos) + "</em>";
                    pos = end + 1;
                } else {
                    htmlLine += "<em>" + line.substr(pos);
                    break;
                }
            } else {
                htmlLine += line[pos];
                ++pos;
            }
        }

        // Process unordered list
        if (line.substr(0, 1) == "-") {
            htmlLine = "<ul><li>" + line.substr(1) + "</li></ul>";
        }

        html += htmlLine + "\n";
    }

    return html;
}
```

---

### 🔍 Example Usage

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::string markdown = 
        "**Bold** and *italic*.\n"
        "# Header 1\n"
        "## Header 2\n"
        "- List item 1\n"
        "- List item 2";

    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

---

### 📌 Output

```html
<strong>Bold</strong> and <em>italic</em>.
<h1>Header 1</h1>
<h2>Header 2</h2>
<ul><li>List item 1</li></ul>
<ul><li>List item 2</li></ul>
```

---

### 📌 Notes

- This is a **simple** parser and does **not** handle nested formatting, escaping, or full Markdown syntax.
- For a **full Markdown parser**, consider using a library like:
  - [markdown.cpp](https://github.com/jgm/markdown)
  - [libmarkdown](https://github.com/jgm/libmarkdown)
  - [Boost.Markdown](https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/release/libs/markdown/)

Let me know if you'd like a more advanced version or support for more Markdown features!

